| 1 | what-happened | On October 1, 2026, the Supreme Court agreed to decide whether the government must hold, while their removal cases are pending, people who are living in the United States and were never admitted. Admitted means the lawful entry of a person into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer. The order takes the case; it does not decide it. The detention statute deems a person present in the United States who has not been admitted an applicant for admission. | scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: THURSDAY, OCTOBER 1, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: RHONEY, ACTING DIR. V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA, RICARDO A.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01: Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: “admission” is defined by statute as “the lawful entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: entry of the alien into the United States after inspection and authorization by an immigration officer.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>uscode-2024-1225: An alien present in the United States who has not been admitted; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1225.txt<br>uscode-2024-1225: shall be deemed for purposes of this chapter an applicant for admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1225.txt |
| 2 | what-happened | The bond statute says the government may keep an arrested person in custody or may release that person on bond. A hearing is a chance at release, not release itself. An immigration judge found that Ricardo Aparecido Barbosa da Cunha presents no danger to persons or property and no risk of flight, and released him on bond. If the government wins, people in his situation cannot be released on bond under the detention statute. The Second Circuit wrote that if the detention statute applies, he "must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond". | uscode-2024-1226: may continue to detain the arrested alien; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1226.txt<br>uscode-2024-1226: may release the alien on; data/sources/immigration-detention/uscode-2024-title8-1226.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: presents no danger to persons or property or risk of flight, released him on bond.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: must be detained during the removal process with no opportunity for release on bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
| 3 | what-happened | For about thirty years, by the Second Circuit's account, people who entered without inspection could ask for bond. Entered without inspection, in a 1997 rule, means present without having been admitted or paroled. The practice did not cover a person caught while entering or shortly afterward, or a person held on a criminal or terrorism ground. On July 10, 2025, a memorandum from the commissioner of Customs and Border Protection said the detention statute covers them instead. On September 5, 2025, the Board of Immigration Appeals said immigration judges lack authority to grant them bond. On September 26, 2025, officers arrested him while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts. An immigration judge denied him a bond hearing. A district court ordered a bond hearing or release within ten days. On April 28, 2026, the Second Circuit ruled the other way from the Board. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Executive Branch practice over thirty years; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06: Despite being applicants for admission, aliens who are present without having been admitted or paroled (formerly referred to as aliens who entered without inspection) will be eligible for bond and bond redetermination.; data/sources/immigration-detention/fr-detention-rule-1997-03-06.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: detain noncitizens on certain criminal or terrorism-related grounds; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18: on July 10, 2025; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-notice-2025-09-18.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: FROM: Rodney S. Scott; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05: Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: arrested Petitioner while he was driving to work in Norwood, Massachusetts; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner requested a bond hearing, which an immigration judge denied based on a determination that he was subject to mandatory detention; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: The district court granted the petition and ordered the government to either provide a bond hearing or release Petitioner within ten days.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
| 4 | what-happened | Before 1996, the procedures for detaining and expelling a person depended largely on whether the person had physically entered the United States. Someone who entered illegally without being caught was eligible for bond. Someone who presented for inspection at a port of entry was to be detained unless clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to enter, or unless granted parole. In 1996 Congress enacted the removal and detention provisions this case concerns. The Justice Department's petition says Congress acted to correct that difference, between people who had entered illegally and people who presented for inspection at a port of entry. Congress replaced the defined term entry with admission. Members of Congress described lawful admission, rather than physical entry, as the pivotal factor. The government contends that Barbosa da Cunha is subject to mandatory detention because he is an applicant for admission who is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted. It argues that "the ordinary meanings of the phrases 'applicant for admission' and 'seeking admission' are the same." | doj-petition-26-104: Before 1996, the applicable procedures for detaining and expelling an alien under the INA depended largely on whether the alien had physically “entered” the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Under that physical-entry-based regime, aliens who managed to evade detection and physically “enter” the United States illegally were subject to deportation proceedings and eligible for bond.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Conversely, aliens who presented themselves for inspection at a port of entry on American soil were deemed not to have “entered” the United States.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Those aliens therefore were subject to exclusion proceedings, and to mandatory detention during such proceedings.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: except those “clearly and beyond a doubt entitled” to enter; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: “shall be detained” for further inquiry, unless they were granted parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: This case concerns several INA provisions addressing removal and detention that were enacted in 1996 as part of the Illegal Immigration Reform and Immigrant Responsibility Act; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: In IIRIRA, Congress acted to correct that disparity.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Congress replaced the defined term “entry” with the term; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: Instead of making physical entry into the United States the touchstone of an alien’s status, the “pivotal factor” became “whether or not the alien has been lawfully admitted.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: The government contends that Petitioner is subject to mandatory detention because he is an “applicant for admission” who “is not clearly and beyond a doubt entitled to be admitted.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: the ordinary meanings of the phrases ‘applicant for admission’ and ‘seeking admission’ are the same.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
| 5 | what-happened | In his August 21 response to that petition, he says that in the detention provisions Congress largely kept the earlier scheme. People arrested inside the country, including "those who had entered without admission," remained eligible for release on bond, and people apprehended at the border continued to be detained without the possibility of bond. | scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21: Dated: August 21, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21.txt<br>scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21: In IIRIRA’s detention statutes, Congress largely preserved the preexisting detention scheme; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21.txt<br>scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21: those who had entered without admission, remained eligible for release on bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21.txt<br>scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21: Those apprehended at the border continued to be detained without possibility of bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21.txt |
| 6 | what-happened | The Second Circuit held that he may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization. He was already living here. The court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone already in the United States who entered without inspection and was not caught while entering or shortly afterward. It affirmed the district court's order, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole, and the parole statute allows that release "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit". | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt |
