| 8 | what-happened | He entered around 2005, lived in the United States for more than twenty years, applied for asylum in 2016, and held a work permit. He has never been arrested for or charged with a crime. Eight appeals courts held that the bond statute applies to noncitizens inside the country, like the people who brought those cases, and that they must be given a bond hearing: the First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits. On September 10, 2026, the Fourth Circuit joined them. The Fifth and Eighth Circuits held that the detention statute applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted, and that they must be detained without bond. | ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: in or around 2005; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: lived in the United States for more than twenty years after entering the country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: applied for asylum in 2016 and was granted a valid work permit.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: has never been arrested for or charged with a crime.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: Decided: September 10, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The First, Second, Third, Sixth, Seventh, Ninth, Tenth, and Eleventh Circuits concluded that Section 1226 applies to noncitizens inside our country; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: like the Petitioners, and that they must thus be provided a bond hearing.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: Today we join the overwhelming majority of circuit courts of appeal that have considered this question; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: The Fifth and Eighth Circuits ruled, on the other hand, that Section 1225 applies to all noncitizens who have not been admitted to the United States; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: they must be detained without bond; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt |
| 9 | the-catch | CBS's tens of thousands CBS News wrote that the Court would consider whether the administration can detain tens of thousands of immigrants during removal proceedings without bond hearings. No court or agency record gives the number of people detained because of the July 2025 change. The Fourth Circuit says district courts sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases, which counts court cases, not people in detention. | cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01: detain tens of thousands of immigrants during their removal proceedings without access to bond hearings; data/sources/coverage/cbs-detention-grant-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-order-list-2026-10-01: The petitions for writs of certiorari are granted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-order-list-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05: Petition GRANTED.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-rhoney-2026-10-05.txt<br>ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28: in July 2025, the government changed that long-settled practice; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28.txt<br>bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05: Immigration Judges lack authority to hear bond requests or to grant bond to aliens who are present in the United States without admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/bia-yajure-hurtado-2025-09-05.txt<br>cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10: section 235 of the Immigration and Nationality Act (INA), rather than section 236, is the applicable immigration detention authority for all applicants for admission.; data/sources/immigration-detention/cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10.txt<br>ca4-bond-2026-09-10: sided with the petitioners in over twenty thousand individual cases; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca4-bond-2026-09-10.txt |
