| 11 | where-this-sits | On September 25, 2026, the full Second Circuit refused to rehear the case. Judge Steven J. Menashi dissented, arguing that the detention statute applies and that Barbosa da Cunha must be detained without a bond hearing. Judge Matthew A. Schwartz voted against rehearing while writing that he agreed with the dissent's analysis. He wrote that mere substantive disagreement with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing. | ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: 09/25/2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: rehearing in banc is hereby DENIED.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25: dissents by opinion from the denial of rehearing in banc.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-rehearing-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25: The government and Judge Menashi in his dissent from the denial of rehearing en banc argue that Section 1225 applies and that Petitioner must be detained without a bond hearing.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25: Although I agree with the analysis in the dissent, I support the decision to deny rehearing en banc.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt<br>ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25: My “[m]ere substantive disagreement” with the panel opinion does not warrant the time and effort of full court rehearing.; data/sources/immigration-detention/ca2-cunha-enbanc-2026-09-25.txt |
| 12 | unknowns | No day for argument has been set. The Court agreed to decide what the detention statute requires, not whether holding someone without a bond hearing violates the Constitution's guarantee of due process. The government's petition says Lopez-Campos, from the Sixth Circuit, also presents that due-process question. Genalo v. G.M., a related constitutional case set for argument on October 13, was dismissed on September 11. On September 21 the government filed a new petition, Genalo v. D.C., with a response due October 21, and Barbosa da Cunha's lawyer wrote that it presents the same constitutional issues as the dismissed case. On September 14 and September 25 that lawyer asked the Court to grant only the statutory question in this case and in Buenrostro-Mendez. The Court had not granted or denied Lopez-Campos or Genalo v. D.C. on the October 5 dockets. The October 3 docket in Buenrostro-Mendez, from the Fifth Circuit, listed no grant or denial. | scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01: RHONEY V. BARBOSA DA CUNHA; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01: Granted: 10/1/26; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-granted-noted-2026-10-01.txt<br>scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01: Whether 8 U.S.C. 1225(b)(2)(A) mandates the detention pending removal proceedings of aliens who, like respondent, are present in the United States without having been admitted.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-question-presented-2026-10-01.txt<br>doj-petition-26-104: In addition to the statutory question presented in this case, Lopez-Campos also squarely presents the question whether applying Section 1225(b)(2)(A)’s mandatory-detention scheme to such aliens comports with due process.; data/sources/immigration-detention/doj-petition-26-104.txt<br>scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-14: On September 11, 2026, the Court dismissed Genalo v. G.M., No. 25-886, which was to be argued on October 13, 2026.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-14.txt<br>scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-14: the Court should grant only on the statutory issue presented in Buenrostro-Mendez and Rhoney; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-14.txt<br>scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05: SET FOR ARGUMENT on Tuesday, October 13, 2026.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05.txt<br>scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05: Sep 11 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05.txt<br>scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05: Case Dismissed - Rule 46.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-genalo-gm-2026-10-05.txt<br>scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-25: The Government has now filed a petition for certiorari that presents the same constitutional issues as those presented in G.M.; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-25.txt<br>scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-25: it should grant only on the statutory issue presented in Buenrostro-Mendez and Rhoney; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-letter-tan-2026-09-25.txt<br>scotus-docket-genalo-dc-2026-10-05: September 21, 2026; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-genalo-dc-2026-10-05.txt<br>scotus-docket-genalo-dc-2026-10-05: Petition for a writ of certiorari before judgment filed. (Response due October 21, 2026); data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-genalo-dc-2026-10-05.txt<br>scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-05: United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-lopez-campos-2026-10-05.txt<br>scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03: United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit; data/sources/immigration-detention/scotus-docket-buenrostro-2026-10-03.txt |
