Rome Bureau Chief","education":[{"name":"Florida International University, BA in communications"}],"bio":"Anthony Faiola is Rome Bureau Chief for The Washington Post. Since joining the paper in 1994, he has served as bureau chief in Miami, Berlin, London, Tokyo, Buenos Aires and New York and additionally worked as roving correspondent at large. ","expertise":"Rome Bureau Chief, covering Italy, the Vatican, Southern Europe and beyond","type":"author","newsDesk":"Foreign","twitter":"@Anthony_Faiola","isWorkdayLoad":true,"beat":"Foreign Affairs","bio_page":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/anthony-faiola/","last_updated_date":"2026-09-04T09:33:36.524Z","byline":"Anthony Faiola","slug":"anthony-faiola","fuzzySearch":true,"gplus":"https://plus.google.com/116230017616622259792","image":"https://author-service-images-prod-us-east-1.publishing.aws.arc.pub/washpost/36baa259-f9da-4b93-8179-6737d60c398a.png","last_updated":"2018-08-21T14:29:07.340Z","jobProfile":"Bureau Chief, Lead","custom_subdesk_name":"International","languages":"Spanish","longBio":"Anthony Faiola is Rome Bureau Chief for The Washington Post. Faiola joined The Post in 1994 and has reported for the paper from more than 60 countries on six continents and served as bureau chief in Tokyo, Buenos Aires, New York, London, Berlin and Miami. From Washington, he has also covered global economics and the U.S. financial crisis. Faiola graduated from Florida International University (FIU) in 1990 and began his journalism career at the Miami Herald. \n","facebook":"https://www.facebook.com/anthony.faiola","custom_washpost_desk_name_1":"Foreign","custom_washpost_desk_name_2":"International - Europe","author_type":"Staff writer","firstName":"Anthony","books":[],"podcasts":[],"awards":[{"name":"Winner, National Press Foundation’s Innovative Storytelling Award"},{"name":"Winner, Cine Award"},{"name":"Finalist, Overseas Press Award "},{"name":"Winner, Maryland-DC-Virginia Press Association Award"},{"name":"Knight Innovator in Residence at Florida International University"}],"desk":"Foreign","location":"Rome","status":"Active","custom_desk_name":"Other"}}},{"_id":"schmidts","type":"author","version":"0.5.8","name":"Samantha Schmidt","org":"Mexico City","image":{"url":"https://author-service-images-prod-us-east-1.publishing.aws.arc.pub/washpost/87708c69-2121-410e-845a-32af1766bdc3.png","version":"0.5.8"},"description":"Samantha Schmidt is The Washington Post's Mexico City bureau chief, covering Latin America","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/samantha-schmidt/","slug":"samantha-schmidt","social_links":[{"site":"twitter","url":"@schmidtsam7"}],"additional_properties":{"original":{"_id":"schmidts","lastName":"Schmidt","education":[{"name":"Indiana University, BA in journalism and Arabic "}],"role":"Mexico City Bureau Chief","bio":"Samantha Schmidt is The Washington Post's Mexico City bureau chief, covering Latin America","affiliations":"National Association of Hispanic Journalists ","expertise":"Mexico City bureau chief, covering Latin America","type":"author","newsDesk":"Foreign","twitter":"@schmidtsam7","isWorkdayLoad":true,"bio_page":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/samantha-schmidt/","last_updated_date":"2026-09-04T09:37:52.349Z","byline":"Samantha Schmidt","slug":"samantha-schmidt","image":"https://author-service-images-prod-us-east-1.publishing.aws.arc.pub/washpost/87708c69-2121-410e-845a-32af1766bdc3.png","last_updated":"2018-09-13T14:19:00.165Z","jobProfile":"Bureau Chief, Lead","custom_subdesk_name":"International","longBio":"Samantha Schmidt is The Washington Post's Mexico City bureau chief, covering Latin America. She was part of a team honored as a Pulitzer Prize finalist in 2026 for coverage of the Trump administration's mass deportation campaign. She was previously The Post's Bogotá bureau chief, leading coverage across Spanish-speaking South America. Reporting from more than a dozen countries, she has investigated the evolution of organized crime in Latin America and documented political transformations across the region. Before becoming a foreign correspondent, her reporting focused on gender and family issues. She joined The Post in 2016 as an overnight reporter covering news from around the country and the world. She also has worked as a reporting fellow for the New York Times. ","languages":"Spanish, Arabic ","custom_washpost_desk_name_1":"Foreign","custom_washpost_desk_name_2":"International - Americas","author_type":"Staff writer","firstName":"Samantha","books":[],"podcasts":[],"awards":[{"name":"Pulitzer Prize finalist, National Reporting, 2026"},{"name":"Overseas Press Club Award, Best reporting in any medium on Latin America, 2025"},{"name":"International Women's Media Foundation Courage in Journalism Award, Ukraine team coverage, 2023"},{"name":"National Headliner Award, First place for news series, 2020 "},{"name":"NLGJA: The Association of LGBTQ Journalists, Excellence in Long Form Journalism Award, 2020"}],"location":"Mexico City","status":"Active","custom_desk_name":"Other"}}},{"_id":"deyoungkj","type":"author","version":"0.5.8","name":"Karen DeYoung","org":"Washington, D.C.","image":{"url":"https://author-service-images-prod-us-east-1.publishing.aws.arc.pub/washpost/ce79ccb8-4e51-4d45-92a0-c773fd22023e.png","version":"0.5.8"},"description":"Karen DeYoung is associate editor and senior national security correspondent for The Post. In more than three decades at the paper, she has served as bureau chief in Latin America and in London and as correspondent covering the White House, U.S. foreign policy and the intelligence community. ","url":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/karen-deyoung/","slug":"karen-deyoung","social_links":[{"site":"twitter","url":"@karendeyoung1"}],"additional_properties":{"original":{"_id":"deyoungkj","lastName":"DeYoung","role":"Associate editor","education":[{"name":"University of Florida, BS in journalism, 1971"}],"bio":"Karen DeYoung is associate editor and senior national security correspondent for The Post. In more than three decades at the paper, she has served as bureau chief in Latin America and in London and as correspondent covering the White House, U.S. foreign policy and the intelligence community. ","expertise":"Associate editor and senior national security correspondent","type":"author","twitter":"@karendeyoung1","isWorkdayLoad":true,"bio_page":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/people/karen-deyoung/","last_updated_date":"2026-09-04T09:33:11.399Z","byline":"Karen DeYoung","slug":"karen-deyoung","image":"https://author-service-images-prod-us-east-1.publishing.aws.arc.pub/washpost/ce79ccb8-4e51-4d45-92a0-c773fd22023e.png","last_updated":"2018-08-16T23:04:50.951Z","jobProfile":"CW - Independent Contractor","custom_subdesk_name":"P\u0026G - National Security","languages":"Spanish","longBio":"Karen DeYoung is associate editor and senior national security correspondent for The Washington Post. In more than three decades at the paper, she has served as bureau chief in Latin America and London and as correspondent covering the White House, U.S. foreign policy and the intelligence community. She has been assistant managing editor for national news, national editor and foreign editor. She has won numerous awards for national and international reporting and is the author of “Soldier,” a biography of Colin Powell.","custom_washpost_desk_name_1":"Other","custom_washpost_desk_name_2":"News Operations","author_type":"Staff","firstName":"Karen","books":[{"publisher":"N/A","title":"Soldier: The Life of Colin Powell","url":"https://www.amazon.com/gp/product/1400075645?ie=UTF8\u0026tag=thewaspos09-20\u0026camp=1789\u0026linkCode=xm2\u0026creativeASIN=1400075645"}],"podcasts":[],"awards":[{"name":"Sigma Delta Chi Awards for Foreign Correspondence and Investigative Reporting"},{"name":"Maria Moors Cabot Prize for Latin America Reporting"},{"name":"Pulitzer Prize for National Reporting"},{"name":"Inter-American Press Association award"},{"name":"W