57-        <p data-layer="narrative">In his August 21 response to that petition, he says that in the detention provisions Congress largely kept the earlier scheme. People arrested inside the country, including "those who had entered without admission," remained eligible for release on bond, and people apprehended at the border continued to be detained without the possibility of bond.<Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="Dated: August 21, 2026" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="In IIRIRA’s detention statutes, Congress largely preserved the preexisting detention scheme" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="those who had entered without admission, remained eligible for release on bond" /><Cite s="scotus-response-barbosa-da-cunha-2026-08-21" passage="Those apprehended at the border continued to be detained without possibility of bond" /></p>
58-        <p data-layer="narrative" data-answer="4">The Second Circuit held that he may be an applicant for admission but is not seeking admission, because he is not requesting lawful entry after inspection and authorization. He was already living here. The court held that the detention statute does not apply to someone already in the United States who entered without inspection and was not caught while entering or shortly afterward. It affirmed the district court's order, which had required a bond hearing or release. Under the July 10 memorandum, an applicant for admission may not be released from custody except by parole, and the parole statute allows that release "only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit".<Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage={`Petitioner may be an “applicant for admission,” but he is not “seeking admission.”`} /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="because he is not requesting lawful entry into the United States after inspection and authorization" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="Section 1225(b)(2)(A) does not apply to noncitizens, such as Petitioner, who are present in the United States after entering the country without inspection and admission" /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="who were not apprehended while entering the country or shortly thereafter." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="we AFFIRM the district court’s grant of the writ of habeas corpus." /><Cite s="cbp-detention-memo-2025-07-10" passage="may not be released from DHS custody except by INA § 212(d)(5) parole." /><Cite s="ca2-cunha-opinion-2026-04-28" passage="only on a case-by-case basis for urgent humanitarian reasons or significant public benefit" /></p>
